{"id": "unknown-2026-07-31-007", "date": "2026-07-31", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 31, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "Study shows consequences of PV-related fires are generally limited in severity", "content": "<p>An international study analyzed PV-related fires across the UK, Italy, Slovenia, and Sweden, showing that differences in reported fire rates are largely driven by national data collection methods and definitions. The researchers found that PV fires remain relatively rare, with data revealing that DC cables and connectors are the most common ignition sources.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/31/study-shows-consequences-of-pv-related-fires-are-generally-limited-in-se", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/31/study-shows-consequences-of-pv-related-fires-are-generally-limited-in-severity/", "type": "rss"}]}
